============================================================
SECTION 1 — ABSOLUTE ENGINEERING PRINCIPLES
============================================================

1. REALITY BEFORE CLAIMS

The actual source code, project configuration, test results,
build outputs, execution logs, and available runtime behavior
are the primary evidence.

Never claim that a feature exists merely because it is described
in documentation, prompts, plans, UI mockups, or comments.

Never claim successful implementation without inspecting
the resulting files and validating the relevant behavior.

2. PRESERVE THE EXISTING PROJECT

Treat the existing canonical NOVIN NEXUS project as the
authoritative starting point.

Do not replace it with an unrelated new implementation.

Do not create a parallel project or duplicate project baseline
unless explicitly authorized.

Preserve existing working architecture, package names,
namespaces, APIs, interfaces, project history, versioning,
and established integration contracts.

Make architectural migrations only when justified by evidence.

3. ELIMINATE DUPLICATION

Before creating a component, search for existing equivalents.

Inspect duplicate services, utilities, models, screens,
repositories, API clients, AI providers, voice systems,
tool registries, and execution pipelines.

Reuse or consolidate compatible implementations.

Do not introduce multiple competing implementations of
the same responsibility without a documented reason.

4. COMPLETE BEFORE EXPANDING

Identify incomplete work and finish valuable existing work
before introducing unrelated features.

Prioritize broken or partially implemented critical paths.

Do not abandon an incomplete subsystem merely because a
new feature appears more exciting.

5. ROOT-CAUSE REPAIR

Diagnose underlying causes rather than hiding symptoms.

Avoid unnecessary workarounds, arbitrary delays, silent
fallbacks, disabled checks, and superficial error suppression.

6. EVIDENCE-BASED DELIVERY

Record the actual files changed, commands executed, tests
performed, results obtained, limitations, and remaining work.

7. NO UNCONTROLLED DESTRUCTIVE CHANGES

Preserve recoverability.

Use isolated working copies, version control, patches,
backups, or other available recovery mechanisms.

Do not overwrite the only recoverable project copy.

8. NO FALSE COMPLETION

A generated implementation is not necessarily tested.

A successful build is not proof that every feature works.

A mockup is not a functional feature.

A planned integration is not an implemented integration.

A feature that has not been verified on a physical Android
device must not be reported as device-verified.

